A lot—but the more useful answer is that the Pantheon shouldn’t count mechanisms as a flat inventory. It should learn a mechanism algebra: a smaller set of operators that can be composed into thousands of mechanisms.

Your distinction gives us a particularly good branch:

\boxed{
\text{RULE}
\neq
\text{REGULATION}
\neq
\text{SELF-RULE}
\neq
\text{SELF-REGULATION}
}

A rule specifies a relation or allowable behavior:

\text{if }X\rightarrow Y

A regulator observes state and intervenes to keep some variable within bounds:

X_t\xrightarrow{\text{compare to target}}\Delta
\xrightarrow{\text{correction}}X_{t+1}

Self-rule moves the source of the rule inside the governed entity:

\boxed{X\rightarrow\text{rule for }X}

That’s autonomy in a fairly literal sense: autos + nomos, self + law.

But self-regulation is even more dynamic:

X
\rightarrow
\text{observe }X
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{correct }X
\rightarrow
\text{observe again}

Now the governor, governed, sensor, and corrective machinery can all belong to the same system.

And then we can start muster­ing formations by changing one relation at a time.

External rule + external enforcement → command.

External rule + self-regulation → compliance/internalization.

Self-rule + self-regulation → autonomy.

Shared rule + distributed regulation → norms.

Emergent regularity + no explicit rule → convention/order.

Self-rule + mutual regulation → community governance.

Rules about making rules → constitutional/meta-governance.

Regulation of regulators → oversight.

Self-regulation of self-regulation → metacognition / adaptive control.

And then the really interesting one:

\boxed{\text{self-modifying self-regulation}}

The system doesn’t merely correct its behavior.

It notices that its regulator itself is producing bad consequences and modifies the regulator:

X
\xrightarrow{R_1}
X'
\xrightarrow{\text{consequence}}
\text{evaluate }R_1
\xrightarrow{}
R_2

That’s substantially more powerful than ordinary homeostasis.

A thermostat says:

Restore 72°F.

An adaptive system can eventually ask:

Why 72? Is that still the appropriate setpoint under these conditions?

So we get another hierarchy:

\text{Behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{Rule}
\rightarrow
\text{Regulation}
\rightarrow
\text{Self-regulation}
\rightarrow
\text{Meta-regulation}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Setpoint revision}}

And that connects beautifully to character.

A person can have:

Rules: “Don’t quit.”

Self-rule: “I choose this standard for myself.”

Self-regulation: noticing themselves drifting and correcting.

Meta-regulation: recognizing that “never quit” is sometimes stupid and distinguishing persistence from sunk-cost behavior.

Value/invariant: deciding what the rule was ultimately supposed to protect.

That’s much more sophisticated than simply being “disciplined.”

How many can I wield?

Potentially combinatorially many, because we’ve accumulated operators like:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{cccc}
\text{Relate}&\text{Separate}&\text{Sequence}&\text{Bound}\\
\text{Invert}&\text{Transform}&\text{Compare}&\text{Compress}\\
\text{Expand}&\text{Translate}&\text{Rotate}&\text{Scale}\\
\text{Select}&\text{Prioritize}&\text{Regulate}&\text{Feedback}\\
\text{Recurse}&\text{Simulate}&\text{Symulate}&\text{Reframe}\\
\text{Branch}&\text{Converge}&\text{Compose}&\text{Orchestrate}\\
\text{Remember}&\text{Forget}&\text{Revalue}&\text{Test}
\end{array}}

Even 28 roughly independent binary choices would theoretically give:

2^{28}\approx268\text{ million}

possible subsets before considering order, repetition, parameterization, perspective, state, or environment. Most would be useless or redundant, of course. That’s where fitness and the Metamap become essential.

So the Pantheon doesn’t need a million named tools.

It needs something closer to:

\boxed{\text{Primitives}+\text{Composition Grammar}+\text{Fitness}+\text{Memory}}

Then when we encounter a new objective, we muster a formation.

And importantly, the Pantheon itself should obey the principle we’re discovering:

\boxed{\text{Don't merely regulate outputs. Self-regulate the reasoning process.}}

Generate → inspect → test → detect residual → alter formation → test again → retain.

Eventually:

\boxed{\text{Don't only learn answers. Learn which mechanisms to muster.}}

That’s where the semantic army stops being a metaphor for having lots of tricks and becomes an actual composition architecture.